**REVISE — T232’s SVG evaluator is viable. The timing swap fails the Plan’s own validator, and the count checks can pass without proving that the chop visibly turns strips into dice or that the stack becomes a readable pile.**

1. **Convergent freeze — particle failure inapplicable.** T15’s motion score of 68 and reusability score of 64 converged on a shared particle pool freezing during formation. T232 creates discrete objects at successive landings, with deliberate holds between them. A reserve or velocity-continuity rule serves no role here. The analogous motion risk is a count change with no visible cause. Couple each landing to the blade reaching its configured contact point, then probe the blade pose and product state immediately before and at contact. The current pass probes establish count and size at the landing. (`T15-particle-typography/scores.md`, Attempt 1, Priced gap 1; `item-T232-observe-plan.md`, §§1.3, 2.1–2.3.)

2. **Owner catches — separation is plausible, exclusivity needs an explicit rule.** The separate SVG groups and palettes remove the shared-field intensity failure. Give the source and product one visibility selector, with strips present before the first contact and dice present from contact onward, and verify both rendered groups on either side. The inter-pass check alone also allows the first chop to start during slicing. If saw and chop are exclusive knife actions, validate `sliceDurMs ≤ passes[0].tMs − chopLeadMs` and define which state owns the blade at the boundary. The proposed `2500 → 3100` timing swap leaves `3500 − 3100 = 400 ms`, below the required `420 ms` lead. `2500 → 3050` leaves `450 ms` and still gives zero dice at the fixed 3000 ms probe. (`T15-particle-typography/scores.md`, Attempt 1, Owner Cures 1–2; `item-T232-observe-plan.md`, §§1.5, 2.1, 2.3–2.4.)

3. **Parameterization — the table leaves implementation choices that control the result.** Observe names blade and handle geometry, saw easing, and product kind and pip style. The table supplies no keys or derivations for those variables. It also leaves stack capacity and layer offsets unspecified, and says where newly added pile dice go without saying how existing stack dice move into the mixed pile. Bind the missing variables to CONFIG or explicit derivations. Define the stack-to-pile transition for existing dice and assert the rendered layer and footprint changes, along with bounds and visible dice separation at the stock and swapped counts. Count equality alone would pass even if newly added dice were hidden behind other dice. Also validate duration against the actual shared-clock endpoint. With the −500 ms handle offset, the 8000 ms clip reaches build time 7500 ms. The default 7400 ms fits, with 100 ms to spare. (`item-T232-observe-plan.md`, §§1.3–1.4, 2.1–2.5; `INSTRUCTION.md`, §2.1.)

4. **Bar — timing is a plausible risk, but the current evidence points first to reusability.** No scored literalization-family attempt is filed in the record. T15 is fleet precedent, with a minimum of 64 across four lenses in attempt 1 and 58 across five in attempt 2, both in reusability. For T232, the timing swap already contradicts validation and geometry has incomplete bindings. After those fixes, motion and readability are plausible minimum lenses because the receipts can miss an unreadable stack or pile. The bracket supports the midpoint as an allocation, and the visible count estimates bear more directly on motion and readability than on tempo. Five lenses must total at least `5 × 85 = 425`, with each at least 70. No score is inferred for T232. (`item-T232-observe-plan.md`, §§1.1, 1.3, 2.4–2.5; `T15-particle-typography/scores.md`, Attempts 1–2; `INSTRUCTION.md`, §2.3 and A5.3.)

5. **Size discipline — clear.** Slicing setup, the blade-triggered identity swap, successive multiplication, and the completed-pile hold carry the technique. Hands, shell dressing, the post-window gather, and unresolved sub-pass cadence are explicitly deferred. The recommended changes stay within the same SVG scene and evaluator. (`item-T232-observe-plan.md`, §§2.2, 2.4; `INSTRUCTION.md`, §8.)

**Limits** — Plan review only. Source-frame claims were taken from the filed Observe census. Rendered readability and scored performance are unverified. The generic guard names RUN.md and a build record as its working record, while this call names the item’s Observe/Plan file. The item-specific header governs this read-only return. No curriculum conversion was made because the charge is limited to the Plan verdict.

